export default "Estrogen dominance gets used loosely in wellness content, but it describes something specific and measurable: too much estrogen relative to progesterone, across part or all of the cycle.\n\nThe framing matters because there are two distinct ways to arrive at estrogen dominance. The first is true excess: estrogen is objectively high. The second, and more common, is relative imbalance: progesterone is too low to balance out estrogen levels that are technically within range. Treatment approaches differ, so getting this distinction right matters.\n\n## Where Estrogen Dominates: The Follicular Phase\n\nEstrogen is the dominant hormone from menstruation through ovulation. It rebuilds the uterine lining, stimulates follicle development, and drives the LH surge that triggers ovulation. Without estrogen, the cycle does not function.\n\nThe problem with estrogen dominance is that this naturally high-estrogen phase becomes exaggerated, or the relative dominance extends into the luteal phase when progesterone should be taking over. This timing tells you a lot: symptoms that peak in the week before ovulation, or that do not ease after ovulation, suggest the estrogen signal is not being properly counterbalanced.\n\n## The Core Symptom Pattern\n\n**Heavy periods with clots**\n\nAn estrogen-dominant environment stimulates endometrial growth without adequate progesterone to stabilize it. The resulting lining is thicker and more vascular than normal, which is why menstrual flow is heavier and why clots form. Passing clots larger than a quarter, soaking a menstrual product in under an hour, or periods lasting more than 7 days are the measurable markers.\n\n**Fibrocystic or tender breasts**\n\nEstrogen stimulates breast tissue proliferation. In an estrogen-dominant environment, breast tissue becomes more reactive: tender, sometimes lumpy, with cysts that are detectable by self-examination. This tenderness often appears in the week before ovulation when estrogen is peaking and may improve after ovulation when progesterone takes over. In severe cases it persists through the whole cycle.\n\n**Mood instability in the first half of the cycle**\n\nEstrogen's effect on serotonin and dopamine signaling means high estrogen can initially produce elevated mood and energy, but estrogen excess can shift this toward irritability, anxiety, and emotional reactivity. When symptoms are concentrated in the follicular phase rather than the premenstrual window, estrogen is the more likely driver than progesterone insufficiency.\n\n**Bloating**\n\nEstrogen promotes water retention and, at high levels, can slow gastrointestinal motility. Cycle-correlated bloating that peaks around ovulation is a common estrogen dominance complaint. This is distinct from the progesterone-related bloating that appears in the luteal phase.\n\n**Conditions that grow in an estrogen-rich environment**\n\nFibroids, endometriosis, and ovarian cysts are all estrogen-sensitive. Their presence does not prove estrogen dominance, but their growth and symptom severity often track with estrogen levels. If you have any of these conditions and are managing worsening symptoms, the estrogen-progesterone balance is worth evaluating.\n\n## How Tracking Data Reveals the Pattern\n\nThe cycle-phase timing of your symptoms is the most useful data you can generate. Log each symptom with its cycle day, not just \"premenstrual,\" and after 2 to 3 cycles, look for the pattern:\n\n- Breast tenderness that peaks before ovulation points toward follicular-phase estrogen\n- Bloating that's worst around Days 12 to 14 is a classic estrogen peak signal\n- Heavy flow with significant clotting suggests inadequate progesterone to stabilize the lining\n- Mood instability or headaches at mid-cycle correspond with the ovulatory estrogen surge\n\nCompare this to the low-progesterone pattern, where symptoms cluster in Days 15 to 28 and ease with menstruation.\n\n## Two Paths to the Same Result\n\n**Path 1: High estrogen**\nEnvironmental estrogen exposure from xenoestrogens in plastics, pesticides, and certain personal care products; impaired liver metabolism of estrogens; excess adipose tissue, which produces estrogen; or medical conditions like polycystic ovaries with multiple follicles producing estrogen.\n\n**Path 2: Low progesterone**\nAnovulatory cycles (no ovulation means no corpus luteum means no progesterone), poor-quality ovulation, luteal phase defect, chronic stress, thyroid dysfunction. Even with normal estrogen, insufficient progesterone creates a relative dominance.\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nTracking symptom timing precisely, by cycle day rather than by vague proximity to your period, is what separates actionable pattern data from noise. Floriva logs symptoms against cycle phase so you can see whether your bloating and breast tenderness peak in the follicular phase (estrogen-dominant signal) or the late luteal phase (progesterone-deficiency signal).\n\nThat distinction does not give you a diagnosis. What it gives you is a documented pattern you can bring to a provider who can then order the appropriate tests, typically a Day 3 FSH/estradiol panel for follicular estrogen levels and a Day 21 progesterone for luteal adequacy.\n\n## When to Seek Evaluation\n\nIf you have heavy periods lasting over 7 days, periods with large clots, confirmed fibroids or endometriosis, or a cycle-correlated symptom pattern that is progressing, get a hormonal panel. Estrogen dominance is treatable. The treatment approach depends on whether the driver is high estrogen, low progesterone, or both.";
